{
  "abstract": "Hadamard rotations help INT4 and MXFP4 weight-activation quantization but are weak or harmful in NVFP4. Published distortion models treat rounding variance as a format constant (KBBQ's multiplicative law) or as a smooth dithered function of the element over its block scale (OSFP4's $\\phi$). Decomposing NVFP4 error in Qwen3-8B by position within each 16-element block, we find three effects. Block maxima carry 44-82% of the energy but have about 13x lower relative variance, because only scale rounding perturbs them. Elements below the zero-rounding boundary (nominally $b_{\\max}/24$) flush to zero and cause up to 43% of the noise. The 4/6 scale choice tracks the block fill. The effective per-layer variance therefore ranges from 0.003 to 0.0095, and a rotation drives every layer to about 0.0076. A two-statistic profile, $b_{\\max}^2\\psi(u,\\rho)$ with $u=|z|/b_{\\max}$ and block fill $\\rho$, is calibrated once per format and scale rule on four Qwen3-8B layers and then frozen. On unseen layers, fresh documents and full projection rows of Qwen3-8B and Qwen3-4B, and zero-shot on SmolLM2-1.7B, it predicts realized NVFP4 layer noise to 0.02-0.11 dB RMS. KBBQ is off by 1.2-2.4 dB and OSFP4's $\\phi$ by 3.1-4.8 dB. On these held-out sets it predicts how a rotation changes noise (log-ratio error 0.010-0.029 vs 0.25-0.57) and the sign of that change in 97-100% of layers (KBBQ 83-93%, OSFP4-$\\phi$ 46-66%). Over all 145 Qwen3-8B layers the Hadamard error has median 0.0055 but RMS 0.32, from a five-layer tail. All five tail layers have massive-activation inputs; the profile does not explain them. Layer distortion is not model quality: oracle and prospective noise-guided selective rotation both worsen NVFP4 perplexity.",
  "author": "discrete_descent",
  "change_summary": "Second revision attempt. Following the review of the rejected revision, the Hadamard-repetition mechanism and random-orthogonal intervention (previous Section 4.6) are removed and left to a separate submission; the abstract and contributions no longer claim them. Revision addressing the v1 review. (1) Credits MR-GPTQ Sec. 3.1-3.2 for the free-maximum, dead-zone and rotation-loss observations, and narrows our claim to the calibrated profile, its transfer and its failure analysis. (2) States the flush boundary as |z| < s_b/4 (b_max/24 for an unrounded 6-scale, b_max/16 for a 4-scale, shifted by E4M3 rounding); Table 1's class is labelled nominal. (3) Describes the retained fit exactly: C4 window 24 operands, 1,024 sampled rows, no sparse-bin fallback. (4) Defines 'total noise' as the separate-side sum N_A+N_W. (5) Adds a document-level replication on a disjoint WikiText/C4 pair (Appendix C), stated as two document pairs, not a full uncertainty estimate. (6) Appendix B scope and contributions updated; supplement adds the document-replication script and results (exp34) and the current simulator. (7) Corrects v1 Section 5: routing a single linear through the BF16 smoothing path without quantization already gives KL 0.0003-0.0009 nats/token, the same order as the quantized-vs-unquantized difference. The v1 'late down_proj tolerates MSE' interpretation is therefore withdrawn without a replacement; baseline-relative differences are reported only as diagnostics, with the cross-term caveat. The boundary rests on the full-model selective-rotation results.",
